Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangrove forests, act as natural buffers against storm surges and high winds. Their effectiveness relies on two primary physical mechanisms: frictional drag and wave attenuation.

First, the dense vegetation in wetlands creates significant surface roughness. As storm surge waters flow through these plants, the stems and leaves generate friction, slowing the water’s velocity. This reduction in speed decreases the kinetic energy of the surge, meaning that when the water eventually reaches inland infrastructure, it exerts less force against buildings and levees. Second, wetlands attenuate wave energy. The complex root systems and